('Dave Munichiello','General Partner','GV',array['$500K - $1M','$1M - $3M','$3M - $10M','$10M - $50M']::text[],array['AI','BioTech','Enterprise','FinTech','Health & Hospital Services','Health IT','IoT','Robotics','Crypto/Blockchain','B2C/E-Commerce','Consumer','DeepTech']::text[],array['Seed','Series A','Series B+']::text[],true,'Pixie; Snorkel AI; Ahana; Segment; SambaNova Systems; ThousandEyes; GitLab; Mode.net; Cockroach Labs; Jet; Slack','dave@gv.com',array['US/Canada','Europe']::text[],'https://twitter.com/davemuni','https://www.linkedin.com/in/dave-munichiello-8467a93/','Dave Munichiello leads the firm’s digital investing team, spanning its consumer, enterprise, and frontier practice areas. Dave is himself deeply curious about data platforms, data science, developer tools, infrastructure, and enterprise software. His investments include Bugsnag (sold to SmartBear), Cockroach, CoreOS (sold to RedHat), DeterminedAI (sold to HPE), GitLab (IPO Nasdaq: GTLB), Jet.com (sold to Walmart), Lattice.io (sold to Apple), Modular, Pixie (sold to NewRelic), Plaid, RedPanda, SambaNova, Segment (sold to Twilio), Slack (IPO, then sold to Salesforce), Snorkel, Upbound, and Wonder.','Palo Alto, California','2023-04-21T09:13:21.000Z'::timestamptz),
('Dave Samuel','Founding General Partner','Freestyle Capital',array['$1M - $3M']::text[],array['Generalist','Consumer']::text[],array['Pre-seed','Seed','Series A']::text[],true,'Airtable; Snapdocs',null,array['US/Canada']::text[],'https://twitter.com/dsamuel','https://www.linkedin.com/in/thedjdave/','He’s a hustling entrepreneur at heart and has been running businesses since he was a kid. At nine years old, he started his first job, a paper route for Waterville Morning Sentinel newspaper in Maine. From there he went on to found Thriller Designs in 5th grade and Sounds Unlimited, a mobile DJ service, in 6th grade, where he DJ’d for 10yrs.  After graduating from MIT and working at Oracle for one year, he co-founded Spinner, the 1st internet radio service, in 1995 (acquired by AOL for $320 million in 1999) and Crackle, an internet video platform, in 2004 (acquired by Sony for $65 million in 2006).','San Francisco, California','2023-04-21T09:21:25.000Z'::timestamptz),
